    "message": {
     "content": "### Executive Summary\n**Do not accept as-is, but do not walk away yet\u2014you should negotiate.**\n\nThis is a solid, above-median baseline offer for Google L6 PM, but because Google recruited you while you are happy and well-compensated at Meta, **you hold maximum leverage**. Accepting an offer with a base salary cut and zero sign-on bonus leaves significant money on the table.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Math: Meta IC6 vs. Google L6\n\n| Component | Meta (Current) | Google Offer | Analysis |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **$5k drop** (band ceiling is $271k) |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | +$11,000 |\n| **Equity (Annual Avg)** | ~$250,000/yr | $270,000/yr ($1.08M / 4) | +$20,000/yr average |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | Standard for L6 is $50k\u2013$100k+ |\n| **4-Yr Average TC** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **+5% increase** |\n\n#### Understanding Google\u2019s Vesting Schedule (The \"Front-Loaded Cliff\")\nGoogle generally does **not** vest equity evenly at 25%/year. Google standardly uses front-loaded vesting (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**):\n\n* **Year 1:** ~$356k\u2013$410k equity $\\rightarrow$ **Total Year 1 Comp: ~$638k\u2013$692k**\n* **Year 2:** ~$345k\u2013$356k equity $\\rightarrow$ **Total Year 2 Comp: ~$627k\u2013$638k**\n* **Year 3:** ~$216k\u2013$237k equity $\\rightarrow$ **Total Year 3 Comp: ~$498k\u2013$519k**\n* **Year 4:** ~$108k\u2013$130k equity $\\rightarrow$ **Total Year 4 Comp: ~$390k\u2013$412k**\n\n**Takeaway:** Your first two years at Google will feel very lucrative, but by Year 3 and Year 4 you face a steep drop. While Google grants annual equity refreshes that stack, they are performance-dependent, market-dependent, and not guaranteed.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Gaps & Weaknesses in the Offer\n\n1. **Lower Base Pay:** Offering $235k when you already make $240k is an immediate discount. Their posted range goes up to $271k, so they have room.\n2. **Missing Sign-on Bonus:** Leaving Meta means forfeiting upcoming unvested equity and performance cycle momentum. A sign-on bonus is the standard tool used to bridge this friction.\n3. **Low Total Compensation Delta:** A ~5% average annual increase is not compelling enough to justify the career friction and risk of leaving a team where you are performing and happy.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What Exactly to Ask For\n\nCounter with a package anchored near the upper quartile of the L6 PM band:\n\n* **Base Salary: $255,000 \u2013 $260,000** (Ask for $260,000)\n  * *Rationale:* Matches/exceeds your Meta base and sits safely below the posted $271k cap.\n* **Equity (GSUs): $1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000 over 4 years**\n  * *Rationale:* Bumps your average annual equity to ~$312k\u2013$325k/yr, protecting you against Year 3/4 drop-offs.\n* **Sign-on Bonus: $75,000 \u2013 $100,000**\n  * *Rationale:* Offsets unvested equity you leave behind at Meta ($200k in the next 12 months) and makes switching worthwhile.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Negotiate (Script & Positioning)\n\nMaintain an appreciative, firm, and high-leverage tone:\n\n> *\"Thank you for extending this offer\u2014I enjoyed meeting the team and I'm excited about the scope of the role. Because I wasn't actively looking and am in a great spot with my current team at Meta, total compensation is the primary factor in deciding whether to make the jump.*\n>\n> *Looking at the numbers, the base salary represents a step down from my current $240k base, and the 4-year average compensation is very close to my current run-rate of $526k, especially without a sign-on bonus to offset the unvested equity I would leave behind over the next 12 months.*\n>\n> *If we can adjust the package to **$260,000 base**, **$1.25M in GSUs**, and an **$80,000 sign-on bonus**, this would be an easy decision for me and I\u2019d be thrilled to sign immediately.\"*\n\n### Final Recommendation\n* **If they meet you near your targets** (~$250k+ base, $1.2M+ equity, ~$50k\u2013$80k sign-on), it turns into a strong offer ($700k+ Year 1) worth taking if you want fresh scope.\n* **If they refuse to move on all fronts**, **walk away.** Staying at Meta\u2014where you already have high context, a higher base, and steady momentum\u2014is the better financial and career risk-adjusted move.",
